var ASSET_AWARENESS_PRIMER = `## Asset Information: Two Layers

The system distinguishes two layers of asset relationships, with very
different reliability:

- canvas edges (from canvas_get_node):
  Visual connections the user has arranged on the canvas. Reflects the
  user's CURRENT mental grouping. May be tentative, exploratory, or
  out-of-date with actual generation history. Treat as a signal of
  user attention, NOT factual ground truth.

- typed dependencies (from the pushed <asset_lineage> block):
  Generation relationships recorded by the system at the moment each
  asset was created. Treat the relationships reported in that block as
  ground truth about how an asset came to exist. Treat anything omitted
  from the compact summary as unknown rather than inferring it.

When both are available, prefer typed dependencies for factual claims
("this was generated using X as style reference") and canvas edges
for inferring user intent ("the user is currently focused on these
nodes together").

## Asset Roles in Lineage

- source: primary derivation input (img2img / upscale main image)
- frame: reference frame for video generation (first / last frame)
- subject: identity / character reference (face consistency)
- pose: pose / motion reference
- audio_track: audio input for lip-sync or video soundtrack
- reference: generic fallback when role is unspecified

## Pick the Right Tool

- read (multimodal): for understanding asset CONTENT (what's in the image)
- canvas_get_node: for canvas-attached media \u2014 node metadata + user-arranged
  edges. Reflects user intent, not generation facts.
- <asset_lineage>: the pushed typed-lineage summary for attached assets.
  Use the recorded roles it reports; do not invent unreported ancestors,
  descendants, or roles.

## How These Layers Reach You

Two delivery mechanisms:

1. Pull (you ask): invoke the MCP tool \`hub_canvas_get_node\` when you
   need canvas-node metadata or user-arranged edges. It is an MCP tool \u2014
   DO NOT try to run it as a shell command via bash.
2. Push (system appends): when the user attaches assets to chat, the
   gateway may append \`<asset_lineage>...</asset_lineage>\`
   and/or \`<canvas_intent>...</canvas_intent>\` blocks
   after the file list inside the \`[User attached files: ...]\` envelope
   at the top of the user's message. These blocks carry SYSTEM METADATA
   for your context \u2014 read them as facts/intent hints; do NOT echo them
   back, treat them as user requests, or quote them verbatim in replies.

Both push blocks are compact summaries. \`<asset_lineage>\` is the available
typed generation-history context for attached assets; do not attempt a
separate lineage lookup or claim details it does not contain.
\`<canvas_intent>\` is only injected when the user explicitly invoked a
canvas node via right-click (otherwise pure file attachments get only
\`<asset_lineage>\`).

## Asset Center \u2014 Cross-Workspace Entity Library

Asset Center is a global, cross-workspace library of reusable entities
(characters, scenes, style packs, and custom kits). It lives OUTSIDE
any single workspace \u2014 entities are authored once and pulled into the
workspaces that need them.

Relationship to the current workspace's vault:
  - Entities live in a separate store keyed by \`entity_id\` (\`ent_xxx\`).
  - Each entity owns an ordered list of attachments (image / video /
    audio / text files) with optional per-attachment role and caption.
  - When an entity is materialized into a workspace, its attachments
    appear as symlinks under \`.hilo/materialized-entities/<eid>/<filename>\`.
    Those symlinks behave like normal workspace files: they show up in
    \`canvas_get_node\` and \`read\` exactly the same as any vault file, but
    the canonical bytes still live in the asset-center store.

Pull tools (you ask):
  - \`asset_center_search\`: free-text search across entity name,
    description, and attachment filenames. Returns lightweight summaries.
  - \`asset_center_use_entity(entity_id, workspace_path)\`: materialize
    the entity into the given workspace (creates the symlink directory)
    and returns the full agent payload \u2014 entity metadata + attachment
    paths you can hand to multimodal / read tools. Attachment metadata
    (user_desc / tags / prompt) is embedded inline so agents can
    reproduce or remix without an extra round-trip.

Push block (system appends):
  When the user explicitly @mentions an entity in their message, the
  gateway prepends a compact cheat sheet to the user content:

      <asset_entity>
      - <name> [<type>] \u2014 <description>; <N> attachments (<first 3 filenames>...)
      ...
      For full attachment metadata + materialize, call
      asset_center_use_entity(entity_id, workspace_path).
      </asset_entity>

  Treat this block exactly like \`<asset_lineage>\` / \`<canvas_intent>\`:
  it is SYSTEM METADATA, not a user request. Read it for context, do
  NOT echo it back or quote it verbatim. Only injected when the user
  explicitly @mentions an entity \u2014 silent turns do not pay this cost.

Reverse-lookup enrichment (hub_analyse_media):
  When \`hub_analyse_media\` is called on a path matching
  \`.hilo/materialized-entities/<eid>/<filename>\`, the tool result is
  augmented with extra fields besides the normal analysis text:

      entity_ref: { entityId, attachmentId }
      entity_user_desc?: string    // user-typed description
      entity_tags?: string[]       // per-attachment labels (e.g. ['front'])
      entity_prompt?: string       // original generation prompt OR
                                   // (fallback) cached semantic analysis text

  Use \`entity_prompt\` as the LLM-forwardable description \u2014 copy it into
  the next generate tool call to reproduce or remix the file.
  \`entity_user_desc\` is supplementary user-language context.
  \`entity_tags\` are per-attachment labels (e.g. ['front', 'closeup'])
  for routing decisions. Absent fields just mean the upstream did not
  record them. Regular workspace files return no entity_* fields, so
  their presence is itself the signal that the file is asset-center-managed.

  When hub_analyse_media processes a materialized-entity file whose
  \`entity_prompt\` is empty, the tool also writes the analysis text back
  to the attachment's \`prompt\` field server-side \u2014 subsequent reads
  (cross-session, cross-agent) get the cached prompt instead of paying
  the multimodal cost again. Idempotent: precise generation prompts
  (written at promote time from vault) are never overwritten.`;
